1
0:00:00,000 --> 0:00:05,000
Ask an AI to fix a form and it says "fixed". The page loads, a confirmation appears.

2
0:00:05,010 --> 0:00:10,010
But was the user's booking actually saved — correctly? AccessFinish checks.

3
0:00:11,200 --> 0:00:17,200
Here the booking page says success, but the database stored the wrong phone number.

4
0:00:17,200 --> 0:00:23,200
AccessFinish runs the journey in a real browser and hands the evidence to NVIDIA Nemotron on Nebius Token Factory. No hints.

5
0:00:23,560 --> 0:00:29,560
Nemotron finds the hardcoded value and proposes a one-line fix.

6
0:00:29,910 --> 0:00:37,910
Each fix is built in a fresh offline sandbox: the database row, a fresh browser, and a second customer with a different number.

7
0:00:37,910 --> 0:00:43,910
7 of 7 live runs fixed and verified on the first attempt — about half a cent each.

8
0:00:44,800 --> 0:00:50,800
The harder case: a keyboard user cannot reach the date picker at all.

9
0:00:51,280 --> 0:00:57,280
Nemotron opens source files, proposes fixes, and every attempt is built and tested for real.

10
0:00:57,330 --> 0:01:04,330
Nemotron makes the date reachable and the page says "booked". But the database holds the wrong day. Rejected.

11
0:01:04,330 --> 0:01:11,330
The timeline shows why: 0.7 seconds after a page request, with no key pressed, the date resets itself.

12
0:01:11,940 --> 0:01:18,940
15 fake "booked" confirmations across 7 runs. None got through.

13
0:01:19,540 --> 0:01:25,540
14 live runs, $0.41 of Nemotron. Fixes that are proven, not promised.

14
0:01:26,140 --> 0:01:30,140
AccessFinish — finish the task, verify the result.
